## Solution

Let $F = \mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{p}, \sqrt{q})$. Since $p, q$ are distinct primes, $\sqrt{q} \notin \mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{p})$ (otherwise $F=\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{p})$ would have $\sqrt{q}$, forcing $\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{q})\subseteq\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{p})$, impossible for distinct squarefree radicands). Hence $[F:\mathbb{Q}]=4$, with $\mathbb{Q}$-basis $\{1,\sqrt{p},\sqrt{q},\sqrt{pq}\}$.

### Candidate basis

Since $p\equiv 1\pmod 4$ and $q\equiv 1\pmod 4$, the rings of integers of the quadratic subfields are
$$\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{p})}=\mathbb{Z}\!\left[\tfrac{1+\sqrt{p}}{2}\right],\qquad \mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{q})}=\mathbb{Z}\!\left[\tfrac{1+\sqrt{q}}{2}\right].$$

Set
$$\alpha=\frac{1+\sqrt{p}}{2},\qquad \beta=\frac{1+\sqrt{q}}{2},\qquad \gamma=\alpha\beta=\frac{(1+\sqrt{p})(1+\sqrt{q})}{4}=\frac{1+\sqrt{p}+\sqrt{q}+\sqrt{pq}}{4}.$$

I claim a $\mathbb{Z}$-basis of $\mathcal{O}_F$ is
$$\boxed{\left\{\,1,\ \frac{1+\sqrt{p}}{2},\ \frac{1+\sqrt{q}}{2},\ \frac{1+\sqrt{p}+\sqrt{q}+\sqrt{pq}}{4}\,\right\}.}$$

### Integrality

$\alpha$ and $\beta$ are integral (they satisfy $x^2-x+\frac{1-p}{2}\cdot 2$... precisely $\alpha$ satisfies $x^2-x+\frac{1-p}{4}=0$, i.e. $x^2-x-\frac{p-1}{4}=0$; since $p\equiv1\pmod4$, $\frac{p-1}{4}\in\mathbb{Z}$, so $\alpha$ is an algebraic integer; similarly $\beta$). Their product $\gamma=\alpha\beta$ is a product of algebraic integers, hence integral. So $\mathcal{O}:=\mathbb{Z}[\alpha,\beta]=\mathbb{Z}\cdot 1\oplus\mathbb{Z}\alpha\oplus\mathbb{Z}\beta\oplus\mathbb{Z}\gamma$ is an order in $F$ (it is a rank-$4$ $\mathbb{Z}$-lattice since $\{1,\alpha,\beta,\gamma\}$ is a $\mathbb{Q}$-basis: $\gamma$ involves $\sqrt{pq}$ which is independent of $\{1,\sqrt{p},\sqrt{q}\}$).

### Discriminant of the order

Let $\sigma_1,\sigma_2,\sigma_3,\sigma_4$ be the four embeddings, sending $(\sqrt{p},\sqrt{q})$ to $(\pm\sqrt{p},\pm\sqrt{q})$. The matrix $M=(\sigma_i(b_j))$ with columns $b\in\{1,\alpha,\beta,\gamma\}$ factors as a Kronecker product $M=A\otimes B$, where
$$A=\begin{pmatrix}1&(1+\sqrt{p})/2\\[2pt]1&(1-\sqrt{p})/2\end{pmatrix},\qquad B=\begin{pmatrix}1&(1+\sqrt{q})/2\\[2pt]1&(1-\sqrt{q})/2\end{pmatrix}.$$
Then $\det M=(\det A)^2(\det B)^2$. Now $\det A=\frac{1-\sqrt{p}}{2}-\frac{1+\sqrt{p}}{2}=-\sqrt{p}$, so $(\det A)^2=p$; similarly $(\det B)^2=q$. Thus
$$\operatorname{disc}(\mathcal{O})=(\det M)^2=(pq)^2=p^2q^2.$$

### Field discriminant

The field $F$ is biquadratic with three quadratic subfields $\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{p})$, $\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{q})$, $\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{pq})$. By the standard biquadratic discriminant formula,
$$\operatorname{disc}(F)=\operatorname{disc}(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{p}))\cdot\operatorname{disc}(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{q}))\cdot\operatorname{disc}(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{pq})).$$
Since $p\equiv q\equiv pq\equiv 1\pmod 4$, each quadratic subfield has discriminant equal to its (squarefree) radicand: $p$, $q$, $pq$ respectively. Hence
$$\operatorname{disc}(F)=p\cdot q\cdot pq=p^2q^2.$$

### Conclusion

For the order $\mathcal{O}\subseteq\mathcal{O}_F$ we have $\operatorname{disc}(\mathcal{O})=[\mathcal{O}_F:\mathcal{O}]^2\operatorname{disc}(\mathcal{O}_F)$. Both discriminants equal $p^2q^2$, so $[\mathcal{O}_F:\mathcal{O}]=1$, i.e. $\mathcal{O}=\mathcal{O}_F$. Therefore the displayed set is a $\mathbb{Z}$-basis of $\mathcal{O}_F=\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{p},\sqrt{q})$.

